 @pandemicneetbux2110  
Intel is known to have quite bad low tier atom and celeron cpu while the atom had over clocking which made it run hot like a toaster.  The pentiums are now scrapped from the production line but they ran hot for a reason.  AMD had a time when everything was meant to be quad core but majority of all programs were still using one core and later on AMD produced dual cores and cluster cores. The dual cores were promoted to be using multi threading but later in experts told that multi core functions were a breach of security since a mirror OS could be used to create a backdoor and that pretty much nailed the coffin on multi core CPU and instead what we got today are cluster cores who work like a single core with many processors. 
In every step Intel been doing is copying AMD. When AMD introduced quad Core Intel started to produce quad core, even if the technicalities of performance with these cpu compared to the old pentiums haven't changed much because of technological limitations.  What the new cpu become better is at reducing heat and energy consumption. Today they say it should be X Celsius but before that 100c was normal operation. To really damage the card it need to reach the melting point and that is above 100c. Any card who get harmed by lower temp is a damaged card who was already broken at first day.

 @robertagren9360  Well running it hot all the time regardless is not going to be good for it longer term. I didn't know that about true multicore, well that's pretty dumb though considering all the technical security flaws could've kept us back generations besides which everyone knows that Intel CPUs are basically backdoored from the start by NSA (or so the story goes, hardware level bypass ability conspiracy theory blahblah). 

But I think it also pretty much doesn't matter about copying, because even if they did it's about who actually does it better. The PC world is known for this, especially case design. Who was it, Thermaltake that always made those cases with great ideas but horrible implemented so all the competitors stole it and did it better? Something according to that logic. Or considering the fact that AMD has been accused of "copying" nVidia, despite the fact that AMD frankly usually makes better hardware for better pricing. Before anybody bitches about this go and check MSRP and then compare all the RDNA card lineup to everything RTX; nVidia blows now. Alternatively, it may not even be something worth copying, like Hairworks.

But in terms of the actual cores, I really don't care as much as AMD fans do because I'm just not using it for anything like that. All that singlethreading nonsense has continually given Intel a huge gaming edge, which when you're running all those cores at once it gets hot af anyway. I still do believe that the average gamer should be "fine" on a quadcore, provided they haven't been running background tasks, and that's where the realy advantages lay for me, is I tried running some game with firefox running a music video in the background on a Intel 4th gen mobile and it just shits itself. On my desktop octacore I routinely have like 3 different launchers, Radeon software, tabbed to hell firefox with videos running etc. and it basically doesn't impact my game at all. So there are other advantages for gamers I've pointed out before, it's just disappointing we don't have more games taking advantage of all those cores, with like 1-4 pegged at 100% while the others aren't doing much.

